        {
            "id": "15848726",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "15:10, 9 November 2014",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(8,161 bytes)",
            "change": 73,
            "summary": "(Enlarging and centering Steps photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Obtain your grains, seeds, nuts, beans'''...something to grind to a pulp.''' Practically anything can be turned into flour -- think quinoa, popcorn (that's right), and peas to the more traditional options like rice, wheat, oats, and barley. Fresh, whole wheat berries, rye berries, whole oats and the like can often be found at health food stores, sold in bulk. They'll be white, russet, purple or amber-colored.<ref name=bread>http://www.breadexperience.com/home-milling.html</ref> And it's cheaper by volume than the pre-made stuff, too!<br><br>[[Image:Make Flour Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#If you're going for wheat flour, know what's best for your cooking needs. Each kind lends itself to a different use. Spelt, Emmer and Einkorn grains are on the comeback and are healthy versions of wheat, too. For yeast breads, hard red (winter or spring) wheat are best.<br><br>[[Image:Make Flour Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Choose your grinding mechanism. If you'd like to spend hours cranking for your daily forearm workout, you're more than welcome. Or you could throw the seeds/berries/nuts/beans in your blender/food processor/coffee grinder and let it do the work for you. If you do use some sort of electric device, the higher powered it is, the finer your flour will be.<br><br>[[Image:Make Flour Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Place the good stuff into your mill/blender. Make the amount you plan to use right now -- fresh flour can go bad very quickly. Fill the mechanism only about halfway full so there's room for it to blend away.<br><br>[[Image:Make Flour Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Grind away. If you're using a mill, turn the crank until all of the grain has been processed through it. If you're using a blender, select the highest setting to blend the grain for about 30 seconds. Then turn it off, remove the lid, and stir with a rubber spatula. After stirring, place the lid back on and blend some more.<br><br>[[Image:Make Flour Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Continue to crank your mill or blend your grains until the flour reaches the texture you want. You can check on this by sifting the mixture you have into a bowl and surveying it up close. Touch it to make sure it has the right consistency (wash your hands thoroughly first!) and if it doesn't, run it again.<br><br>[[Image:Make Flour Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Once you're satisfied with your flour, pour it into a resealable bag or container. You may have to use more than one if you've made a lot of flour, but keeping it fresh will definitely pay off in the long-term. And there you have it: ready-made flour for the dough of your dreams!<br><br>[[Image:Make Flour Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#If making bulk amounts, keep it in the fridge or freezer. Whole wheat flour will go rancid especially fast, clocking in at only a few months if put in a cabinet. If it changes color or smells bad (which it won't do when kept cold), don't hesitate to throw it away.<ref name=what>http://whatscookingamerica.net/Bread/FlourTypes.htm</ref><br><br>[[Image:Make Flour Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Experiment with your flour first. You may find that your homemade flour has a much different taste than you expected and acts quite differently when cooked (that's because it's super fresh). So don't use it straight away if you're looking for a gold medal at the fair. Experiment first.<br><br>[[Image:Make Flour Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Obtain your grains, seeds, nuts, beans'''...something to grind to a pulp.''' Practically anything can be turned into flour -- think quinoa, popcorn (that's right), and peas to the more traditional options like rice, wheat, oats, and barley. Fresh, whole wheat berries, rye berries, whole oats and the like can often be found at health food stores, sold in bulk. They'll be white, russet, purple or amber-colored.<ref name=bread>http://www.breadexperience.com/home-milling.html</ref> And it's cheaper by volume than the pre-made stuff, too![[Image:Make Flour Step 1.jpg|center|300px]]",
                    "#If you're going for wheat flour, know what's best for your cooking needs. Each kind lends itself to a different use. Spelt, Emmer and Einkorn grains are on the comeback and are healthy versions of wheat, too. For yeast breads, hard red (winter or spring) wheat are best.[[Image:Make Flour Step 2.jpg|center|300px]]",
                    "#Choose your grinding mechanism. If you'd like to spend hours cranking for your daily forearm workout, you're more than welcome. Or you could throw the seeds/berries/nuts/beans in your blender/food processor/coffee grinder and let it do the work for you. If you do use some sort of electric device, the higher powered it is, the finer your flour will be.[[Image:Make Flour Step 3.jpg|center|300px]]",
                    "#Place the good stuff into your mill/blender. Make the amount you plan to use right now -- fresh flour can go bad very quickly. Fill the mechanism only about halfway full so there's room for it to blend away.[[Image:Make Flour Step 4.jpg|center|300px]]",
                    "#Grind away. If you're using a mill, turn the crank until all of the grain has been processed through it. If you're using a blender, select the highest setting to blend the grain for about 30 seconds. Then turn it off, remove the lid, and stir with a rubber spatula. After stirring, place the lid back on and blend some more.[[Image:Make Flour Step 5.jpg|center|300px]]",
                    "#Continue to crank your mill or blend your grains until the flour reaches the texture you want. You can check on this by sifting the mixture you have into a bowl and surveying it up close. Touch it to make sure it has the right consistency (wash your hands thoroughly first!) and if it doesn't, run it again.[[Image:Make Flour Step 6.jpg|center|300px]]",
                    "#Once you're satisfied with your flour, pour it into a resealable bag or container. You may have to use more than one if you've made a lot of flour, but keeping it fresh will definitely pay off in the long-term. And there you have it: ready-made flour for the dough of your dreams![[Image:Make Flour Step 7.jpg|center|300px]]",
                    "#If making bulk amounts, keep it in the fridge or freezer. Whole wheat flour will go rancid especially fast, clocking in at only a few months if put in a cabinet. If it changes color or smells bad (which it won't do when kept cold), don't hesitate to throw it away.<ref name=what>http://whatscookingamerica.net/Bread/FlourTypes.htm</ref>[[Image:Make Flour Step 8.jpg|center|300px]]",
                    "#Experiment with your flour first. You may find that your homemade flour has a much different taste than you expected and acts quite differently when cooked (that's because it's super fresh). So don't use it straight away if you're looking for a gold medal at the fair. Experiment first.[[Image:Make Flour Step 9.jpg|center|300px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
