                    "nbgj bn  trntm , sold in bulk. They'll be white, russet, purple or amber-colored.<ref name=bread>http://www.breadexperience.com/home-milling.html</ref> And it's cheaper by volume than the pre-made stuff, too!"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Most of us probably think that flour is cultivated somewhere by elves that work long hours in a factory. The truth of the matter is that you can make it yourself in ''seconds''. Why use that processed crap that's been losing vitamins for weeks on the shelves when you can get the good stuff now? Keep reading for detailed information on how to mak ejje  w xu ejw iw   wn3d nceu  ejc  ecekm ecjj he   ee c33h",
                    "e flour!",
                    "",
                    "#Obtain your grains, seeds, nuts, beans'''...something to grind to a pulp.''' Practically anything can be turned into flour -- think quinoa, popcorn (that's right), and peas to the more traditional options like rice, wheat, oats, and barley. Fresh, whole wheat berries, rye berries, whole oats and the like can often be found at health food stores, sold in bulk. They'll be white, russet, purple or amber-colored.<ref name=bread>http://www.breadexperience.com/home-milling.html</ref> And it's cheaper by volume than the pre-made stuff, too!"
                ]
