            "reverted_by": "BR",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "[[Image:Make Rice Flour Step 5.jpg|right|250px]]Making flour at home is a very different approach from making other foods. For one thing, the process can involve growing the ingredients yourself. For another, the cooking part of it doesn't involve any pans or ovens, only a hand grain mill or high-performance blender. Of course you can always purchase the ingredients directly and skip to the \"preparation\" section of this guide, but if you choose to plant your own seeds, you will truly obtain the full flour-making experience. Read on to learn all about the magic of flour and how to concoct it!",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "=== Cultivation ===",
                    "#Buy whole grain seeds. These can be wheat, barley, rye, any kind of grain you like. Some will be more difficult to find than others, but you should be able to locate most of the varieties you want without much trouble.",
                    "#Groom your gardening space. If you already have a flower bed or an empty patch of soil available, go ahead and use that! Eliminate any weeds and spread out fertilizer and compost. Also make sure that you'll be able to water it easily—don't raise it too high off the ground (unless it's right under your window) and don't position it too far from your water source. It may not seem like it now, but stretching a hose twenty feet every day can get to be a real hassle.",
                    "#Plant the seeds. You can scatter them randomly or you can make orderly little rows and label each one with the type of grain it is. The placement of the seeds doesn't matter as much as looking after them once they're in the ground.",
                    "#Care for the seeds. Water them regularly and see that they receive enough sunlight. Grains can take a long time to germinate, especially in a harsh climate. Wheat requires three to four months to reach its full potential, and other grains take even longer. Give it time and pay close attention to your grain's progression to get an idea of when it will be ready for harvesting.",
                    "#Gather your crops. Pull them out or cut them with an agricultural tool like a scythe, if you get your hands on one. Wash and dry it and you're all set to start making your flour!",
                    "=== Preparation ===",
                    "#Decide on the type of whole grain you want to use. If you've been growing your own, now is the time to harvest it. If you plan on purchasing it, try your local health food store or order it in bulk online. You can put more than one type of grain in a batch of flour, too, so don't be afraid to get creative with combinations!",
                    "#Chop up the grain and put it into your mill/blender. Add it in proportionate amounts depending on what you want it to taste like. For your standard, generic wheat flour, use wheat; for a heartier mix, use oats; etc.",
                    "#If you're using a mill, turn the crank until all of the grain has been processed through it. Alternately, if you're using a blender, select the highest setting to blend the grain for a minute or two, then turn it off, remove the lid, and stir with a rubber spatula. After stirring, place the lid back on and blend some more.",
                    "#Repeat step 3 of this section with whichever tool you're using. Continue to crank your mill or blend your grains until the flour reaches the texture you want. You can check on this by sifting the mixture you have into a bowl and surveying it up close. Touch it to make sure it has the right consistency (wash your hands thoroughly first!) and if it doesn't, run it again.",
                    "#Once you're satisfied with your flour, pour it into a sealable bag or container. You may have to use more than one if you've made a lot of flour, but keeping it fresh will definitely pay off in the long-term. And there you have it: ready-made flour for the dough of your dreams!",
                    "",
                    "== Things You'll Need ==",
                    "For planting:",
                    "*Area of soil",
                    "*Fertilizer",
                    "*Compost",
                    "*Sprinklers, hose, or watering can",
                    "*Scythe (optional)",
                    "For preparing:",
                    "*Hand grain mill or high-performance blender (such as a Vitamix or a Blendtec Total Blender)",
                    "*Rubber spatula",
                    "*Sifter",
                    "*Bowl",
                    "*Container(s)",
                    "*Note that different types of grains contain different nutrients. Do some research before picking out the one(s) you want to put in your flour.",
                    "*When watering your grains, be sure to distribute the water evenly. For example, if you use a hose, don't just lay it down and let it run for a few minutes. Place your thumb over the spout to create a spray and move it back and forth across your crops.",
                    "*If you're not achieving the desired consistency from your mill, try using your blender too to see if it makes a difference. Even though a hand mill's intended purpose is to grind up grains to make flour, a blender can sometimes get the job done more effectively.",
                    "*Adding a teaspoon of lemon juice for every two cups of grain will help make the flour rise better.",
                    "*Wheat flour is extremely flammable. Never have flour out near an open flame!",
                    "*Like other crops, grains can be prone to contaminants and natural toxins, so wash them well before consuming.",
                    "*[[Make Coconut Flour|How to Make Coconut Flour]]",
                    "*[[Make Almond Flour or Meal|How to Make Almond Flour or Meal]]",
                    "*[[Make Rice Flour|How to Make Rice Flour]]",
                    "*[[Make Self Rising Flour|How to Make Self Rising Flour]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "[[Image:Wheatfield   Lillehammer, Norway 4333.jpg|thumb|200px]]Making flour is not for the faint of heart. It is a long, ongoing process in which the wheat is planted, later [[Grow Your Own Food|harvested]], and then ground to make flour.The earliest methods used for producing flour all involved grinding grain between stones. Below are the steps needed to make flour.",
                    "==  Steps ==",
                    "===Planting===",
                    "#  Purchase wheat [[Winter Sow Seeds Outdoors|seeds]]. Search catalogs or head to the natural food store to purchase seeds.",
                    "#  [[Find Free Compost Ingredients|Amend soil]], if needed. Wheat seeds need to be planted in a rich soil.",
                    "#  Plant wheat seed.",
                    "===Cultivation===",
                    "#  [[Fertilize a Garden Cheaply|Fertilize]] crop as required.",
                    "#  [[Start an Organic Garden|Weed]] as needed.",
                    "===Harvest===",
                    "#  Cut the wheat using a scythe or other [[Sharpen a Knife|sharp knife]].",
                    "#  Tie the cut wheat into bundles.",
                    "#  Thresh the wheat by taking a handful of  wheat and beating it against the interior of a barrel or other hard surface. The [[Brew Beer Using All Grain Method|grain]] will fall off the stalks. Discard the stalks.",
                    "#  Remove (Winnow) the wheat from the chaff. To separate the wheat from the chaff toss the wheat from one container to another allowing airflow to remove the wheat from the chaff. Wind or a fan can be used for this purpose.",
                    "===Storage===",
                    "#  Store the dry wheat in an [[Store a Christmas Cake|airtight]] container. Make sure wheat does not become",
                    "===Make the Flour===",
                    "#  Grind the wheat until it reaches the desired fineness. <ref>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mill_%28grinding%29 Wikipedia article on grinding wheat</ref>",
                    "*All-purpose (plain) flour is a combination of both hard and soft flours. It is used for baking both breads and cakes.",
                    "*Note there are different types of wheat based on protein content. Red or winter wheat is normally used for baking breads.",
                    "*Soft wheat is normally used to make cakes and pastries.",
                    "*Wheat flour is flammable. Wheat mills have been known to explode or catch on  fire from just a spark.",
                    "*Wheat is prone to toxins <ref>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ergot Wikipedia Ergot</ref> so use care when growing, harvesting and storing wheat.",
                    "*[[Harvest Wheat|How to Harvest Wheat]]",
                    "",
                    "== Sources and Citations ==",
                    "*http://www.breadinfo.com/floura.shtml",
                    "*http://www.cookingforengineers.com/article/63/Wheat-Flour",
                    "*http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flour",
                    "{{reflist}}"
                ]
            }
        },
